    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://edamontology.org/format_3687">
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://edamontology.org/formats"/>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://edamontology.org/bio"/>
        <repository rdf:resource="https://github.com/ISA-tools/isa-specs"/>
        <oboInOwl:savedBy>2026-06-13 Jonathan Hunter, Philippe Rocca-Serra, Helge Hecht, Matúš Kalaš</oboInOwl:savedBy>
        <rdfs:comment>ISA-Tab extends the MAGE-TAB format (edam:format_3162) and its gene expression-specific focus, but remains compatible with it.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://www.nature.com/documents/scidata-isatab-specification.pdf"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The ISA model consists of three core entities to capture experimental metadata: Investigation, Study, and Assay. The ISA-Tab is a composite format comprised of one i_Investigation.txt file (edam:format_4066), and one or more Study (s_*.txt; edam:format_4067) and Assay (a_*.txt; edam:format_4068) files.</rdfs:comment>
        <created_in>1.13</created_in>
        <skos:editorialNote>TODO when Composite format and its parts (using http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf ?) are ready in EDAM: ISA-Tab with subconcepts, MHD announcement?, ...</skos:editorialNote>
        <documentation rdf:resource="https://isa-specs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/isamodel.html"/>
        <rdfs:comment>ISA-Tab format can be converted to ISA-JSON (edam:format_4069; validated against a set of JSON Schemata defined by the ISA model). From ISA-JSON, a serialisation to RDF (JSON-LD) is possible when using a series of JSON context files.</rdfs:comment>
        <information_standard rdf:resource="https://isa-tools.org/format/specification"/>
        <documentation rdf:resource="https://isa-specs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/isatab.html"/>
        <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.25504/FAIRsharing.53gp75"/>
        <skos:editorialNote>TODO review the relation to MAGE-TAB</skos:editorialNote>
        <rdfs:comment>Based on the Investigation / Study / Assay (ISA) Abstract Model, the tab-delimited ISA-Tab format supports the metadata related to multimodality/multi-omics studies which employ a combination of data acquisition techniques.</rdfs:comment>
        <citation rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.161355"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Investigation Study Assay Tabular</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>ISA-Tab</rdfs:label>
        <ontology_used>Ontologies should be used. Any ontology is accepted, as long as it is correctly recorded.</ontology_used>
        <example rdf:resource="https://github.com/ISA-tools/ISAdatasets/tree/master/tab"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>ISA-Tab stands for Investigation / Study / Assay (ISA) tab-delimited (Tab) format, and it is comprised of metadata describing observational or experimental scientific studies.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <example rdf:resource="https://isa-tools.org/format/examples.html"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://edamontology.org/format_4069">
        <information_standard rdf:resource="https://isa-tools.org/format/specification"/>
        <example rdf:resource="https://github.com/ISA-tools/ISAdatasets/tree/master/json"/>
        <documentation rdf:resource="https://isa-specs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/isajson.html"/>
        <file_extension>json</file_extension>
        <rdfs:comment>The ISA model consists of three core entities to capture experimental metadata: Investigation, Study, and Assay. All ISA-JSON content should fall under one Investigation JSON structure in a single JSON file.</rdfs:comment>
        <citation rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.1038/ng.1054"/>
        <ontology_used>Ontologies should be used. Any ontology is accepted, as long as it is correctly recorded.</ontology_used>
        <rdfs:label>ISA-JSON</rdfs:label>
        <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.25504/FAIRsharing.yhLgTV"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Investigation Study Assay JSON</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <repository rdf:resource="https://github.com/ISA-tools/isa-specs"/>
        <oboInOwl:savedBy>2026-06-15 Jonathan Hunter, Philippe Rocca-Serra, Helge Hecht, Matúš Kalaš</oboInOwl:savedBy>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://edamontology.org/formats"/>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-01-09</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>ISA-JSON stands for Investigation / Study / Assay (ISA) JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) format, and it is comprised of metadata describing observational or experimental scientific studies.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>jonathan-hunter</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://edamontology.org/bio"/>
        <rdfs:comment>ISA-JSON is the JSON serialisation of the Investigation / Study / Assay (ISA) Abstract Model, complementing ISA-Tab (edam:format_3687). It supports the metadata related to multimodality/multi-omics studies which employ a combination of data acquisition techniques. From ISA-JSON, a serialisation to RDF (JSON-LD) is possible when using a series of JSON context files.</rdfs:comment>
        <created_in>1.26</created_in>
        <documentation rdf:resource="https://isa-specs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/isamodel.html"/>
    </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>



